# Unit character assets — drop the Blue Archive character art / models here.
# Served at /units/<name> (this folder is Vite's public root).
#
# The game tries assets in this order and falls back gracefully at each step:
#   1. <key>.glb        -> rigged 3D character (animated; gun optional, below)
#   2. <key>.png        -> camera-facing 2D billboard (static, shown as-is)
#   3. built-in procedural placeholder       (labelled stand-in, always works)
# So the game runs with NO files here; add .png for a flat sprite, or .glb for
# a full 3D model. .glb wins over .png when both are present.
#
# Keys / characters / weapons:
#   saori    -> Unit 1 / Assault Rifle   (M4A1)
#   hoshino  -> Unit 2 / Shotgun         (XM1014)
#   aru      -> Unit 3 / Sniper Rifle    (PSG1)
#   atsuko   -> Unit 4 / Submachine Gun
#
# ============================================================================
# 3D MODELS — <key>.glb  (+ optional <key>_gun.glb)
# ============================================================================
# Upload, per unit (6 files total if you keep guns separate):
#   saori.glb    + saori_gun.glb
#   hoshino.glb  + hoshino_gun.glb
#   aru.glb      + aru_gun.glb
#
# CHARACTER  <key>.glb
#   - Format: binary glTF (.glb), Y-up, real-world-ish proportions. Export
#     UNCOMPRESSED — no Draco / no Meshopt / no KTX2 (the loader has no
#     decoders wired in). A plain "glTF Binary" export from Blender works.
#   - Must be RIGGED with a skeleton. Animations travel inside the same .glb
#     as named clips. The engine picks a clip by case-insensitive SUBSTRING
#     of its name (first match wins):
#         idle    <- "idle", "stand", "wait"
#         walk    <- "walk", "move"
#         sprint  <- "run", "sprint"
#         dodge   <- "dodge", "roll", "evade", "avoid", "step", "dash"
#         fire    <- "fire", "shoot", "attack", "atk", "shot", "skill"
#     So e.g. a clip "Armature|Run01" resolves to sprint. Missing clips
#     degrade: sprint->walk->idle->(first clip in file); fire is optional
#     (locomotion just keeps playing if there's no fire clip).
#   - Auto-fitted in-engine: scaled to a set height, feet planted on the
#     ground line, centered — so the source model's scale/origin don't matter.
#   - Facing: the model is turned to face its current target. If yours ends up
#     facing AWAY, flip UNIT_MODEL_YAW_OFFSET (Math.PI <-> 0) in client/src/main.js.
#
# GUN  <key>_gun.glb   (OPTIONAL)
#   - Skip this file entirely if the character already holds its weapon in the
#     character .glb. Only add it when the gun is a SEPARATE mesh.
#   - The gun is parented to the character's right-hand BONE, so it rides every
#     animation automatically. The hand bone is auto-detected across common
#     rigs (Mixamo / VRoid / Rigify). If detection misses, set `handBone` for
#     that unit in UNIT_MODEL_CONFIG (client/src/main.js) to a substring of the
#     correct bone's name.
#   - Seat the gun in the grip via UNIT_MODEL_CONFIG[<key>].gun = { pos, rot
#     (RADIANS), scale }, expressed in the hand bone's local space. These
#     almost always need hand-tuning per model — start at zeros and nudge until
#     the gun sits in the hand. gun.scale is RELATIVE to the auto-fitted
#     character (start at 1).
#
# ============================================================================
# 2D BILLBOARDS — saori.png / hoshino.png / aru.png   (fallback if no .glb)
# ============================================================================
#   - PNG, TRANSPARENT background (alpha cutout) — cut out the character so
#     there's NO box/background behind it, or it will render as a flat card.
#   - Portrait, full body, feet near the BOTTOM edge (the sprite is anchored at
#     the feet). ~512x768 / 2:3 ratio works well; any portrait aspect is fine
#     (the engine scales to a fixed height and reads the image's own aspect).
#   - The weapon (M4A1 / XM1014 / PSG1) should be drawn into the character art
#     itself — there's no separate weapon model in 2D mode.
#   - Auto-fitted in-engine: scaled to a set height, feet planted — so the
#     source image's pixel size/scale doesn't matter, only the aspect ratio.
